<!--Post: 8082--><!--Time: 1108861630-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST8082"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Audreyamw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Audreyamw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, February 19, 2005 - 5:07 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I&#39;ve been fascinated by some SK who have expressed opinions along the lines of seeing the murders they commit as a kind of self-expression -- a kind of art. <BR> <BR>Katherine Ramsland has an article on John Knowles at Crimelibrary.com, a SK who liked the novel <i>Johnathon Livingston Seagull</i> because he related to the Seagull character, a misfit that breaks away from its flock to explore things no seagull has ever done before, like flying at night.  The Seagull experiences a liberating thrill in doing these things.  This also parallels the writings of Ian Brady &#40;Moors child killer&#41;, who wrote in his autobiography that he had an epiphany as a boy of about 12 years old.  On a family picnic, he wandered off alone and climbed to the top of a hill, where he experienced some sort of feeling of complete autonomy and power -- apparently it dawned on him that he, as an individual, was free of moral responsibility and could do anything he wanted to do.  Whereas most people feel fear and grief when they experience existentialist angst, Brady felt power and nihilism. <BR> <BR>BTK, like Knowles and Brady, and Jack Unterweger &#40;Austrian SK turned autobiographical novelist&#41;, wants his story told.  But why?  And why is he so interested in literature?  Could a psychopath really want to be understood by other people?  Is he desiring self-expression?  Does this mean that he does experience emotions at some level?  BTK certainly seems to be an angry person, not necessarily as completely cold-blooded as SK are thought to be by some researchers. BTK seems to be boiling over with resentment over abuse or neglect he feels he&#39;s suffered at the hands of society.  Maybe his murders are a way of throwing criticism back at society: &#34;See what you&#39;ve done to me?  Let me demonstrate.&#34; Even the role playing during the murders, with a choreographed routine, the mask of an executioner that he wears when he commits the murders -- it&#39;s like a twisted performance art.  <BR> <BR>Some theorists say SK kill to feel power, because in some way at their core they feel completely powerless -- killing is one way for them to feel power and control.  Is BTK motivated by revenge against society, like a one-man terrorist?  Is BTK&#39;s poetry, his autobiography, something akin to Che Guevera&#39;s <i>Motorcycle Diaries</i>?  Is SK art? <BR> <BR>From the sublime to the ridiculous: Paul Bernardo, Canadian sadistic SK, was also oddly artistically creative -- he wanted to be a rap star and wrote rap songs...about rape... like Vanilla Ice but worse. <BR> <BR>What does all this mean? <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 8082-->
<!--Post: 8086--><!--Time: 1108862864-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST8086"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Audreyamw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Audreyamw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, February 19, 2005 - 5:27 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Someone claiming to be BTK wrote this at the AMW message boards today.  Interestingly it also refers to the art angle: <BR> <BR><b>From: __BTK__ 3:40 pm <BR>To: ALL &#40;1 of 2&#41; <BR> <BR>1741.1 <BR> <BR>OK you investegating Blue pigs trying to be police officers. i will make a note to you fans and i will send it into the investigation office at witchita. if you want to make fun of my orational of being a Artist of sort <BR> <BR> <BR> <BR>-BTK </b><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 8086-->
<!--Post: 8121--><!--Time: 1108889770-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST8121"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Louise<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Louise<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, February 20, 2005 - 12:56 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Zodiac also referred to the &#34;Blue pigs.&#34; I doubt that this is an authentic BTK writing, but if it is he might be emulating Zodiac...or is HE Zodiac? I realize this has been discussed before, but if and when BTK is caught the good thing is they can check him out against any Zodiac crimes as well. Hasten that day!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 8121-->
<!--Post: 8126--><!--Time: 1108903528-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST8126"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, February 20, 2005 - 4:45 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->In BTK&#39;s mind, his &#39;works&#39; are pieces of art, in terms of his control of the victims, their pain, their submission to him, and what he considers to be his dominion over life and death. He does not realize that his victims control him, as he can achieve no satisfaction &#40;sexual or otherwise&#41; and no domination  without them. As Helen Morrison states in her book &#34;All serial killers are cut from the same cloth&#34;. There is only so much a killer can do before he starts to repeat what others have done. BTK appears to consider his taunting of the police and media as his works of art as well. He will meet his match if he ever encounters a veteran newsperson who knows the power of the press and TV, and releases information to the public in the tradition and history of those who forged their industry into one of the most powerful entities on the earth. Until then, he will remain hidden in the darkness of public awareness to continue his &#39;works of art&#39;. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 8126-->
<!--Post: 8129--><!--Time: 1108913201-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST8129"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Surfietx<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Surfietx<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, February 20, 2005 - 7:26 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I agree with the art angle.  I started a thread about something else over at AMW, but it produced the same conclusions regarding the art aspect.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 8129-->
<!--Post: 8282--><!--Time: 1109036672-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST8282"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Audreyamw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Audreyamw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, February 21, 2005 - 5:44 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Really good article by Katherine Ramsland on murderer Melvin Rees: <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.crimelibrary.com/serial_killers/predators/melvin_rees/index.html" target=_top>http://www.crimelibrary.com/serial_killers/predato rs/melvin_rees/index.html</a> <BR> <BR>Rees divided his time between murder and touring clubs as a jazz musician.  He often discussed philosophy with a friend, especially the nature of morality.  Ramsland includes an excellent chapter on &#34;The Existentialist Excuse&#34; offered up by a lot of murderers.  This is a great in-depth exploration of the cultural trends that have influenced the way a lot of SK see themselves &#40;and probably how the mainstream media decides to portray them&#41;.  Ah, rebels without a clue...<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 8282-->
